1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,333
Today, we'll be continuing the series of lectures on memory by focusing on what is called episodic memory

2
00:00:10,333 --> 00:00:15,500
and what can happen if this is not working properly.

3
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:21,448
Episodic memory refers to the memory of an event or 'episode'.

4
00:00:21,448 --> 00:00:30,100
Episodic memories allow us to mentally travel back in time to an event from the past.

5
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:35,510
Episodic memories include various details about these events,

6
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:44,300
for example, when an event happened and other information such as the location.

7
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:47,600
To help understand this concept

8
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:52,500
try to remember the last time you ate dinner at a restaurant.

9
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,939
The ability to remember where you ate,

10
00:00:55,939 --> 00:01:03,800
who you were with and the items you ordered are all features of an episodic memory.

11
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:12,000
Episodic memory is distinct from another type of memory called semantic memory.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,630
This is the type of factual memory that we have in common with everyone else -

13
00:01:19,630 --> 00:01:23,700
that is your general knowledge of the world.

14
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,620
To build upon a previous example

15
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:33,850
remembering where you parked your car is an example of episodic memory,

16
00:01:33,850 --> 00:01:37,880
but your understanding of what a car is

17
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:44,000
and how an engine works are examples of semantic memory.

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Unlike episodic memory, semantic memory isn't dependent on recalling personal experiences.

19
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,750
Episodic memory can be thought of as a process

20
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:09,000
with several different steps of memory processing: encoding, consolidation and retrieval.

21
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,680
The initial step is called encoding

22
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:18,643
This involves the process of receiving and registering information,

23
00:02:18,643 --> 00:02:27,000
which is necessary for creating memories of information or events that you experience.

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:39,900
The degree to which you can successfully encode information depends on the level of attention you give to an event while it's actually happening.

25
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:46,400
Being distracted can make effective encoding very difficult.

26
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:55,000
Encoding of episodic memories is also influenced by how you process the event.

27
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,273
For example, if you were introduced to someone called Charlie,

28
00:03:00,273 --> 00:03:06,600
you might make the connection that your uncle has the same name.

29
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:11,540
Future recollection of Charlie's name is much easier

30
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:16,000
if you have a strategy to help you encode it.

31
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:25,200
Memory consolidation, the next step in forming an episodic memory, is the process by

32
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,320
which memories of encoded information are strengthened

33
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:34,400
stabilised and stored to facilitate later retrieval.

34
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:46,900
Consolidation is most effective when the information being stored can be linked to an existing network of information.

35
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:55,100
Consolidation makes it possible for you to store memories for later retrieval indefinitely.

36
00:03:55,100 --> 00:04:01,800
Forming strong memories depends on the frequency with

37
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:04,100
which you try to retrieve them.

38
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:11,800
Memories can fade or become harder to retrieve if they aren't used very often.

39
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:18,522
The last step in forming episodic memories is called retrieval,

40
00:04:18,522 --> 00:04:23,900
which is the conscious recollection of encoded information.

41
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:31,540
Retrieving information from episodic memory depends upon semantic

42
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:36,600
olfactory, auditory and visual factors.

43
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:43,600
These help episodic memory retrieval by acting as a prompt.

44
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:49,280
For example, when recalling where you parked your car

45
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:54,900
you may use the colour of a sign close to where you parked.

46
00:04:54,900 --> 00:05:01,300
You actually have to mentally travel back to the moment you parked.

47
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:06,360
There are a wide range of neurological diseases

48
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:11,200
and conditions that can affect episodic memory.

49
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:18,200
These range from Alzheimer's to schizophrenia to autism.

50
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:27,300
An impairment of episodic memory can have a profound effect on individuals' lives.

51
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:36,591
For example, the symptoms of schizophrenia can be reasonably well controlled by medication; however,

52
00:05:36,591 --> 00:05:43,940
patients' episodic memory may still be impaired and

53
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:49,200
so they are often unable to return to university or work.

54
00:05:49,200 --> 00:06:01,100
Recent studies have shown that computer-assisted games designed to keep the brain active can help improve their episodic memory.

55
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:06,572
Episodic memories can help people connect with others,

56
00:06:06,572 --> 00:06:12,728
for instance by sharing intimate details about their past:

57
00:06:12,728 --> 00:06:18,200
something individuals with autism often have problems with.

58
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:24,600
This may be caused by an absence of a sense of self.

59
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:29,460
This is essential for the storage of episodic memory,

60
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:35,400
and has been found to be impaired in children with autism.

61
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:47,000
Research has shown that treatments that improve memory may also have a positive impact on children's social development.
